WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions for the Rem0te walkthrough (media/walkthrough.mp4, 14:35).
The video is caption-led and has no narration or audio track; these cues are
the on-screen captions, timed from the recorder's own log
(scripts/video-captions.mjs).

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:06.860
Rem0te — Self-hosted remote support and management
for the computers of your customer businesses, built
on RustDesk. — A captioned tour of every feature ·
v0.53

2
00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:16.630
A demo environment: every business, person and
computer here is fictitious

3
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.260
The computers report through a simulated agent:
nothing shown here ran on a real Windows PC

4
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.490
The dashboard: what needs attention now, across
every customer business

5
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:33.170
A site outage is grouped into one alert instead of
one per computer

6
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.900
Counts, customers affected and patch status, each a
link into the detail

7
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.890
Devices: every enrolled computer you may see, with
health, agent and patches

8
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:52.520
Search by name, host, IP, user or business

9
00:00:52.570 --> 00:01:00.020
Filters for business, site, group, online state,
alerts and agent

10
00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:04.840
Views save a filter set, private or shared with the
team

11
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.720
Columns come in at the window widths where they fit

12
00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:19.370
Tick computers for bulk actions: update, reinstall,
tags, groups, export

13
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.050
One computer's workspace: status, the actions you
may take, and a tab per job

14
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:31.500
The header says what is wrong at a glance: alerts,
pending updates, which agent

15
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:38.540
The Tasks panel: everything sent to this computer,
and where each one is

16
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:45.990
Recent tasks stay listed for two weeks, each with
its steps and its result

17
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.770
The Agent card: version, channel, and automatic
agent updates

18
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:57.460
Automatic updates can be set per computer: Inherit,
On or Off

19
00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:03.530
This demo server runs with automatic agent updates
switched off, which wins over the computer's On

20
00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:10.680
Inventory: hardware, operating system, disks and the
logged-on user

21
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:16.960
Performance: processor, memory and disk over up to
30 days

22
00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.260
Software: the catalog, ready to install, and every
installed program

23
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:29.290
Install a package from the catalog: one signed plan
for this computer

24
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:41.480
Remove an installed program: the agent reads the
uninstall entry itself and decides how

25
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:51.270
Rem0te and RustDesk are protected and never offered
for removal

26
00:02:51.320 --> 00:03:03.400
Services: start, stop, restart, or change the
startup type

27
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:12.320
Each change is a signed plan; the agent checks the
service again before it acts

28
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:26.250
Processes: end one process, matched by name and
start time, never a reused PID

29
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.490
Event logs, with Ask AI and Create ticket on any
entry

30
00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:39.180
Ask AI explains an entry in plain language. Here the
provider is a local stand-in

31
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:54.170
Create ticket sends the entry to your PSA, with the
AI explanation if you want it

32
00:03:54.220 --> 00:04:00.740
The ticket number comes back from the PSA (a
loopback stand-in in this demo)

33
00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:05.800
Patches: the signed agent's last Windows Update
scan, and what it offers

34
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:15.310
The Latest install follows an install from the
request to the scan after it

35
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:20.660
One row per update, with Windows' own error code and
what it means

36
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:25.590
Windows wants a restart: Schedule restart warns the
signed-in user first, and never interrupts a session

37
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.870
Scan now, or let the scan two minutes after the
restart refresh the list

38
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:35.480
Updates that install put on the computer wait for
the restart instead of being offered again

39
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.380
Tick the update that failed to try it again

40
00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:49.170
Install now, or Schedule it: a date, a time and a
time zone, up to 14 days ahead

41
00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:58.340
Or start at the next opening of this computer's
patch policy window

42
00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:03.350
Nothing is handed to the computer before then, and
until it starts it can be canceled

43
00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.620
Each computer's Patch policy: Inherit the
company-wide policy, Exclude, or use another policy

44
00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:16.810
It says when the policy's window next opens

45
00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:24.070
Exclude stops automatic patching at once; Install
updates on this tab still works

46
00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.770
Monitoring: this computer's alerts, and which
monitors apply to it

47
00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:37.630
Actions: approved maintenance, like starting the
Print Spooler, with its current state

48
00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.280
Diagnostics: a fixed catalog of read-only checks,
run on the computer

49
00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:44.400
Chat with the person at the computer, in the tray on
their screen

50
00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:49.170
Pictures and files go both ways; a picture is shown
only if its bytes really are one

51
00:05:49.220 --> 00:06:07.380
A built-in emoji picker

52
00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:16.200
Unread replies from every computer collect in the
header's chat inbox

53
00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.120
History: sessions, changes and actions on this
computer, newest first

54
00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:27.950
The number in the top bar is your tasks in progress,
on every page

55
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:36.800
Automation → Tasks: every task on every computer you
can see

56
00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:41.880
Queued for the next check-in, handed out, scheduled,
held, or done

57
00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:47.810
Overdue when it takes longer than its kind usually
does, with what that usually means

58
00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:54.440
A scheduled install, held until its time; it can be
canceled until then

59
00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:01.520
Filter by state and by type

60
00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:26.140
Now the gatehouse install answers. A task you
started finishing shows a toast, wherever you are

61
00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:32.330
Done, with each update's result

62
00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:41.560
Connect opens RustDesk on your computer, configured
for this server

63
00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:48.290
It releases the computer's password, so it asks you
to confirm it is you first

64
00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:57.830
A computer that has gone quiet says so under its
buttons, with a link to reinstall its agent

65
00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:09.940
The same is under More → Reinstall manually
(download link)

66
00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:15.490
The link re-attaches to the same record: history,
site and settings kept, no duplicate

67
00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:27.580
Add device: choose where the computer lands, and who
may connect to it

68
00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:33.890
The business is fixed into the link; the computer
that runs it cannot choose another

69
00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:37.000
Then pick the operating system and make a one-time
install link

70
00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:45.860
Light and dark themes ship with the product

71
00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:50.320
Patching: Windows updates a canary first, then
rings, inside a maintenance window

72
00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:55.480
The pre-flight reads what signed patching needs and
says what is missing

73
00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:01.240
A company-wide policy covers every computer set to
Inherit

74
00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:06.440
The portal builds the rings: a canary that checks in
reliably, then rings of at most 25

75
00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:12.050
A computer that cannot be patched is listed with the
reason

76
00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.810
When computers join or leave, a new revision is
proposed and signed like any policy

77
00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:22.850
An assignable policy covers only the computers set
to use it: here the depots, monthly

78
00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:27.860
Two days after Patch Tuesday, and it says when its
window next opens

79
00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:33.890
Every computer's setting in one place: Inherit,
Exclude or Use policy

80
00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:46.670
A new policy chooses its classes, window, soak
between rings, restart rule and canary

81
00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:59.610
A policy's maintenance window is weekly or monthly

82
00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:06.840
The nth or last weekday of the month, plus up to 14
days: second Tuesday + 2 is the Thursday after Patch
Tuesday

83
00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:12.840
Its next three openings come from the server, worked
out exactly as the approval signer checks them

84
00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:24.350
Or a day of the month, 1 to 28, so it opens every
month, February included

85
00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:39.050
A deployment's next ring can start now or at a time
too

86
00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:46.490
The time becomes the signed plan's start, and the
agent refuses it early. Not sent in this demo

87
00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:51.490
A scheduled ring shows when it starts, and can be
canceled until then

88
00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:57.630
The patch compliance report, by business and by
computer

89
00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.810
The software catalog: approved packages with the
installer's hash pinned

90
00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:21.210
Browse the winget community index and add packages
by selection

91
00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:30.170
The script library: versioned PowerShell, reviewed
before it can run anywhere

92
00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:35.330
Each version shows its content hash and static
checks, and needs approving

93
00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.800
Alerts from every monitor, grouped and filtered

94
00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:48.310
An outage lists the computers in it; acknowledge or
resolve it with a note

95
00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:55.460
Monitors are set globally, per business, site, group
or computer, the most specific winning

96
00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:05.010
Reports: patch status, offline time, alerts, agent
coverage and software

97
00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:07.970
Each one can be exported as CSV, or emailed on a
schedule

98
00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:17.850
Businesses: each customer owns its computers,
people, sites and history

99
00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:23.990
Automatic agent updates per business: Inherit, On,
Off or in its maintenance window

100
00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:33.430
Confirm it is you: choose, function by function,
which changes ask again after sign-in

101
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:45.800
Here the everyday changes are set to Don't ask;
install links and security changes still ask

102
00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:55.920
Some always ask: your password, authenticator and
passkeys, and turning a confirmation off

103
00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:02.360
AI: your own provider and key. This demo answers
from a local stand-in, with a fake key

104
00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:08.160
PSA / ticketing: a connector per business or for the
whole platform

105
00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:13.920
Email: your SMTP relay for alerts and scheduled
reports, to confirmed recipients only

106
00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:19.270
Access control: Platform Admin, Business Owner and
Business User

107
00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:24.650
Each user holds exactly the capabilities granted to
them

108
00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:30.810
Users: invitations, roles, MFA status and
offboarding

109
00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.940
Security: what protects the server, read from the
server itself

110
00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:40.390
Every change is in the audit log, in a hash chain
that shows any deletion

111
00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:57.980
The full documentation is built into the app, with
search

112
00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:06.050
The tray runs on Windows. These are its generated
icons; its menu has not been photographed on Windows

113
00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:15.840
Its menu opens with the business and its IT
provider, and offers a support request only when a
PSA is set up

114
00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.780
Help: short answers for technicians, in the app

115
00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:28.730
Self-hosted. Open source. No RustDesk Pro required.

116
00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:35.130
Rem0te — Self-hosted remote support and management,
built on RustDesk. Install guide, docs and source on
GitHub. — github.com/agit8or1/rem0te · mspreboot.com
